Consider a hypothetical weakly interacting massive particle (WIMP) dark matter candidate with the following precisely specified properties:

(i) rest mass m ≈ 70 GeV/c²;
(ii) spin-independent elastic scattering cross-section on a xenon nucleus of order σ ≈ 10⁻⁴⁸ cm²;
(iii) no non-gravitational interactions other than second-order (loop-level) electroweak gauge couplings to the W and Z bosons.

Determine whether such a particle can be either confirmed or excluded by currently feasible experiments, namely:

(a) direct-detection experiments using ultra-low-background xenon time-projection chambers (with sensitivities at or below the so-called "neutrino fog" floor near 10⁻⁴⁸ cm² for ~70 GeV/c² WIMPs);
(b) high-energy collider searches (e.g., production and missing-energy or diboson signatures at the LHC and proposed successors);
(c) indirect-detection experiments looking for the products of dark-matter annihilation or decay (gamma rays, neutrinos, cosmic-ray antiparticles, etc.).

Provide, with rigorous justification, either an experimental protocol (including required exposure, background rejection, and statistical significance) capable of discovering or ruling out this candidate, or a proof that no presently envisaged experiment of types (a)–(c) can achieve such a confirmation/exclusion.

